---
import StandardPageLayout from "../../layouts/StandardPageLayout.astro";
import FinancialStatementKey from "../../components/FinancialStatementKey.astro";
import Glossary from "../../components/Glossary.astro";
import PageToc from "../../components/PageToc.astro";
import "../../styles/standards-poster.css";
import "../../styles/extended.css";
import metadata from "../../data/standards/indas/indas104.json";
---

<StandardPageLayout
  code={metadata.code}
  standardType={metadata.framework}
  title={metadata.title}
  description={metadata.summary}
  issued={metadata.issued}
  latestAmendment={metadata.latestAmendment}
  status={metadata.status}
  summary={metadata.summary}
  bannerNotice={metadata.bannerNotice}
>
  <Fragment slot="toc">
    <PageToc
      items={[
        { id: "s1", label: "Conceptual Framework Linkage" },
        { id: "s2", label: "Scope & Applicability" },
        { id: "s3", label: "Insurance Risk — The Classification Gateway" },
        { id: "s4", label: "Continuation of Existing Accounting Policies" },
        { id: "s5", label: "The Liability Adequacy Test" },
        { id: "s6", label: "IRDAI's Regulatory Role in Indian Insurance Accounting" },
        { id: "s7", label: "Prohibited Practices" },
        { id: "s8", label: "Embedded Derivatives & Unbundling" },
        { id: "s9", label: "Disclosure" },
        { id: "s10", label: "Judgments & Estimates" },
        { id: "s11", label: "Ind AS 104 vs IFRS 4/IFRS 17 — Full Comparison" },
        { id: "s12", label: "Statement Mapping" },
        { id: "s13", label: "Related Standards" },
        { id: "s14", label: "Glossary" },
      ]}
    />
  </Fragment>
  <div class="wrap">

    <!-- ============ SECTION 1 — CONCEPTUAL FRAMEWORK LINKAGE ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s1"><span class="num">1</span><h2>Conceptual Framework Linkage</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card ci">
        <div class="ctitle ci">Why India's insurance accounting sits in a distinctive, dual-track position</div>
        <div class="principle">India's insurance SECTOR is regulated by IRDAI (Insurance Regulatory and Development Authority of India), which prescribes its OWN detailed financial statement formats and actuarial reserving requirements for LICENSED insurers under the Insurance Act, 1938 and IRDAI regulations. Ind AS 104 was notified as India's converged version of IFRS 4, but its APPLICATION to insurance COMPANIES themselves has been repeatedly DEFERRED by the MCA — meaning most Indian insurers continue preparing statutory financial statements under IRDAI's own framework rather than full Ind AS. Ind AS 104 remains directly relevant primarily to NON-insurance entities holding insurance contracts, or for LIMITED purposes.</div>
        <div class="elabel">HOW IND AS 104 MAPS TO THE FRAMEWORK</div>
        <div class="check-list">
          <span><strong style="font-weight:500">Pragmatic sequencing, similar to IFRS 4's original rationale</strong> — just as IFRS 4 was a deliberate interim step pending comprehensive global reform, India's DEFERRAL of full Ind AS for insurers reflects a similar pragmatic sequencing, prioritising REGULATORY (IRDAI) stability for a systemically important, heavily supervised sector</span>
          <span><strong style="font-weight:500">Faithful representation, safeguarded by liability adequacy</strong> — the liability adequacy test (Section 5) provides the SAME minimum reliability floor as under IFRS 4, wherever Ind AS 104 IS applied</span>
          <span><strong style="font-weight:500">A standard in genuine TRANSITION</strong> — India has been actively working toward an Ind AS 117 (IFRS 17-equivalent) implementation roadmap for insurers, meaning Ind AS 104's current relevance is itself somewhat TEMPORARY, similar to how IFRS 4 itself was always understood as interim globally</span>
        </div>
        <div class="note">This is a genuinely IMPORTANT and DISTINCTIVE feature of the Ind AS landscape — unlike Ind AS 101/102/103 (converged with CURRENT IFRS), Ind AS 104 reflects convergence with an OLDER, SUPERSEDED global standard (IFRS 4), since India has not yet completed its transition to an IFRS 17 equivalent.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="card cs">
        <div class="ctitle cs">The dual-track Indian insurance reporting landscape</div>
        <div class="td-table">
          <div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:1.3fr 1fr;"><span>Reporting track</span><span>Applicable framework</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.3fr 1fr;"><span>Statutory returns for licensed Indian insurers</span><span>IRDAI's own prescribed formats and actuarial valuation regulations</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.3fr 1fr;"><span>Insurance contracts held by NON-insurance entities</span><span>Ind AS 104, converged with IFRS 4</span></div>
        </div>
        <div class="callout info callout--chip" style="margin-bottom:0;">ℹ️ This DUAL-TRACK structure is genuinely unique among the Ind AS/IFRS standards examined in this library.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 2 — SCOPE & DEFINITIONS ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s2"><span class="num">2</span><h2>Scope &amp; Applicability</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cw">
        <div class="ctitle cw">What Ind AS 104 covers</div>
        <div class="principle">Ind AS 104 applies to insurance contracts an entity ISSUES (including reinsurance contracts held), and financial instruments with discretionary participation features — IDENTICAL scope structure to IFRS 4.</div>
        <div class="note">In PRACTICE, its most common Indian application is for NON-insurance entities holding certain insurance-linked instruments, or IRDAI-regulated entities to the (limited) extent Ind AS applies to them.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="card cd">
        <div class="ctitle cd">Key definitions you must know</div>
        <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">IRDAI</div><div class="glossary-def">Insurance Regulatory and Development Authority of India — the statutory body regulating and supervising India's insurance sector, prescribing its own financial statement formats for licensed insurers.</div></div>
        <div class="glossary-item" style="border-bottom:none;"><div class="glossary-term">Liability adequacy test</div><div class="glossary-def">Identical concept to IFRS 4 — a minimum reliability check ensuring recognised insurance liabilities are not understated using current cash flow estimates.</div></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 3 — INSURANCE RISK ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s3"><span class="num">3</span><h2>Insurance Risk — The Classification Gateway</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card ci span2">
        <div class="ctitle ci">Identical test to IFRS 4</div>
        <div class="principle">A contract qualifies as an "insurance contract" only if it transfers SIGNIFICANT insurance risk — the SAME test as IFRS 4, with contracts lacking significant insurance risk instead accounted for under Ind AS 109 (the Ind AS equivalent of IFRS 9).</div>
        <div class="worked" style="margin-bottom:0;">
          <div class="wk-title">Example 1: Testing significant insurance risk under Ind AS 104</div>
          <div class="wk-body">An Indian non-insurance holding company holds a group credit-life policy on behalf of employees, paying out a fixed sum assured on death, materially exceeding the policy's cash surrender value.</div>
          <div class="wk-answer">This clearly transfers SIGNIFICANT insurance risk, qualifying for Ind AS 104 treatment (with the underlying insurer, a licensed Indian insurance company, itself reporting under IRDAI's framework for its own statutory purposes) — an identical analysis to how IFRS 4 would classify the same contract globally.</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 4 — CONTINUATION OF EXISTING POLICIES ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s4"><span class="num">4</span><h2>Continuation of Existing Accounting Policies</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cs span2">
        <div class="ctitle cs">Identical interim mechanism to IFRS 4</div>
        <div style="font-size:11px;color:var(--color-text-secondary);line-height:1.6;">Where Ind AS 104 applies, an entity may CONTINUE its existing insurance accounting policies without full IAS 8/Ind AS 8 justification — the SAME explicit carve-out as IFRS 4, subject to the liability adequacy test and prohibited practices (Section 7).</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 5 — LIABILITY ADEQUACY TEST ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s5"><span class="num">5</span><h2>The Liability Adequacy Test</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cd span2">
        <div class="ctitle cd">Identical mechanism to IFRS 4</div>
        <div style="font-size:11px;color:var(--color-text-secondary);line-height:1.6;">An entity assesses, at each reporting date, whether recognised insurance liabilities are ADEQUATE using CURRENT cash flow estimates — any deficiency recognised IMMEDIATELY in profit or loss, mirroring IFRS 4's own minimum reliability floor exactly.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 6 — IRDAI REGULATORY ROLE ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s6"><span class="num">6</span><h2>IRDAI's Regulatory Role in Indian Insurance Accounting</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card ci span2">
        <div class="ctitle ci">A parallel, and in practice DOMINANT, reporting framework</div>
        <div class="principle">IRDAI prescribes DETAILED financial statement formats (per the IRDAI (Preparation of Financial Statements and Auditor's Report of Insurance Companies) Regulations), actuarial VALUATION methodologies for insurance liabilities, and SOLVENCY margin requirements — a comprehensive REGULATORY accounting framework that, for LICENSED Indian insurers, effectively takes PRECEDENCE over Ind AS 104 in practical day-to-day statutory reporting.</div>
        <div class="worked" style="margin-bottom:0;">
          <div class="wk-title">Example 2: IRDAI format vs Ind AS 104 in practice</div>
          <div class="wk-body">A licensed Indian life insurance company prepares its statutory annual financial statements using IRDAI's prescribed Revenue Account, Profit and Loss Account, and Balance Sheet formats, with insurance liabilities valued per IRDAI actuarial regulations.</div>
          <div class="wk-answer">The insurer's PRIMARY statutory reporting obligation is to IRDAI's framework, NOT Ind AS 104 directly — Ind AS 104 becomes most directly relevant for entities OUTSIDE the IRDAI-regulated insurance sector holding insurance-type contracts, or in the context of India's ONGOING roadmap toward eventually notifying a comprehensive Ind AS 117 for insurers themselves.</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 7 — PROHIBITED PRACTICES ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s7"><span class="num">7</span><h2>Prohibited Practices</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cw span2">
        <div class="ctitle cw">Identical prohibitions to IFRS 4</div>
        <div style="font-size:11px;color:var(--color-text-secondary);line-height:1.6;">Ind AS 104 prohibits catastrophe and equalisation provisions for risks NOT yet incurred — an IDENTICAL prohibition to IFRS 4, since these represent future, not present, obligations under the Conceptual Framework's basic liability definition.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 8 — EMBEDDED DERIVATIVES & UNBUNDLING ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s8"><span class="num">8</span><h2>Embedded Derivatives &amp; Unbundling</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cn span2">
        <div class="ctitle cn">Identical treatment to IFRS 4</div>
        <div style="font-size:11px;color:var(--color-text-secondary);line-height:1.6;">Embedded derivatives requiring separation are bifurcated and measured at fair value per Ind AS 109; unbundling of deposit components is permitted but not required, IDENTICAL to IFRS 4's own treatment.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 9 — DISCLOSURE ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s9"><span class="num">9</span><h2>Disclosure Requirements</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cd span2">
        <div class="ctitle cd">Required disclosures — identical structure to IFRS 4</div>
        <div class="check-list">
          <span>Accounting policies for insurance contracts</span>
          <span>Recognised assets, liabilities, income, expense from insurance contracts</span>
          <span>Nature/extent of risks arising from insurance contracts</span>
          <span>Terms/conditions materially affecting amount, timing, uncertainty of future cash flows</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 10 — JUDGMENTS & ESTIMATES ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s10"><span class="num">10</span><h2>Critical Judgments &amp; Estimates</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cn span2">
        <div class="ctitle cn">Where management judgement matters most</div>
        <div class="two-col">
          <div>
            <div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT JUDGEMENTS</div>
            <div class="dot-list">
              <span>Whether significant insurance risk is transferred, determining Ind AS 104 vs Ind AS 109 classification</span>
              <span>For entities within IRDAI's regulatory scope, the interaction between IRDAI's own actuarial regulations and any Ind AS 104 requirements that may apply</span>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div>
            <div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT ESTIMATES</div>
            <div class="dot-list">
              <span>Current cash flow estimates for the liability adequacy test</span>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="callout warn" style="margin-top:8px;margin-bottom:0;">⚠️ India's insurance sector faces a GENUINELY DISTINCTIVE dual regulatory challenge — navigating BOTH IRDAI's detailed statutory framework AND the evolving Ind AS landscape, with FULL Ind AS 117-equivalent convergence still pending as of this poster's preparation.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 11 — IND AS 104 VS IFRS 4/17 ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s11"><span class="num">11</span><h2>Ind AS 104 vs IFRS 4/IFRS 17 — Full Comparison</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card ci span2">
        <div class="ctitle ci">Comprehensive comparison table — a genuinely important time-lag</div>
        <div class="td-table">
          <div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1fr 1fr;"><span>Feature</span><span style="color:var(--color-text-info);">Global IFRS</span><span style="color:var(--color-text-success);">Ind AS 104</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1fr 1fr;"><span>Current global standard</span><span>IFRS 17 (mandatory since 1 January 2023) — comprehensive current-value measurement model</span><span>Ind AS 104 REMAINS in effect, converged with the SUPERSEDED IFRS 4 — India has NOT yet notified an IFRS 17 equivalent</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1fr 1fr;"><span>Measurement approach</span><span>IFRS 17: fulfilment cash flows + Contractual Service Margin, mandatory globally</span><span>Ind AS 104: continuation of existing (largely IRDAI-driven) accounting policies, subject to a liability adequacy floor</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1fr 1fr;"><span>Primary regulatory driver for insurers</span><span>Varies by jurisdiction, generally the accounting standard itself</span><span>IRDAI's own detailed regulatory framework DOMINATES statutory Indian insurer reporting in practice</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1fr 1fr;"><span>Applicability to insurance companies specifically</span><span>IFRS 17 applies DIRECTLY and comprehensively to insurers globally</span><span>MCA has repeatedly DEFERRED full Ind AS applicability to insurance companies themselves</span></div>
        </div>
        <div class="note">This is one of the MOST SIGNIFICANT and consequential lags between Ind AS and current global IFRS across this entire library — while Ind AS 101/102/103/105/106 are converged with CURRENT IFRS counterparts, Ind AS 104 reflects convergence with IFRS 4, a standard the REST of the world moved beyond in 2023. India's insurance accounting convergence roadmap remains an ACTIVE, ongoing area of regulatory development.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 12 — STATEMENT MAPPING ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s12"><span class="num">12</span><h2>Statement Mapping</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cs span2">
        <div class="ctitle cs">Comprehensive event-to-statement mapping table</div>
        <div class="td-table">
          <div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Event / transaction</span><span>Statement(s) affected</span><span>Notes</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Insurance liability recognition (continued policy)</span><span><span class="badge bs">Balance Sheet</span></span><span>Per continued accounting policy, or IRDAI framework for licensed insurers</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Liability adequacy test deficiency</span><span><span class="badge bp">P&amp;L</span> <span class="badge bs">Balance Sheet</span></span><span>Full deficiency recognised immediately</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Embedded derivative bifurcation</span><span><span class="badge bp">P&amp;L</span> <span class="badge bs">Balance Sheet</span></span><span>Fair value per Ind AS 109</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Catastrophe/equalisation provisions</span><span>—</span><span>Prohibited, identical to IFRS 4</span></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 13 — RELATED STANDARDS ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s13"><span class="num">13</span><h2>Related Standards</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cd">
        <div class="ctitle cd">Cross-reference map</div>
        <div class="td-table">
          <div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>Standard</span><span>Connection</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>IFRS 4</span><span>The GLOBAL counterpart standard Ind AS 104 remains converged with (now superseded internationally by IFRS 17)</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>Ind AS 109</span><span>Governs contracts failing the significant insurance risk test</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>IRDAI Regulations</span><span>The dominant statutory reporting framework for licensed Indian insurers, operating largely INDEPENDENTLY of Ind AS 104</span></div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="card cn">
        <div class="ctitle cn">Quick-reference glossary</div>
        <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">IRDAI</div><div class="glossary-def">Insurance Regulatory and Development Authority of India — India's insurance sector regulator, whose own accounting/actuarial framework governs licensed insurers' statutory reporting.</div></div>
        <div class="glossary-item" style="border-bottom:none;"><div class="glossary-term">Ind AS 117 (not yet notified)</div><div class="glossary-def">The anticipated future Ind AS equivalent of IFRS 17, not yet issued by the MCA as of this poster's preparation.</div></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 14 — GLOSSARY ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s14"><span class="num">14</span><h2>Glossary</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <Glossary entries={[
      { term: "IRDAI", definition: "Insurance Regulatory and Development Authority of India — the statutory body regulating and supervising India's insurance sector, prescribing its own financial statement formats for licensed insurers." },
      { term: "Liability adequacy test", definition: "Identical concept to IFRS 4 — a minimum reliability check ensuring recognised insurance liabilities are not understated using current cash flow estimates." },
      { term: "Significant insurance risk", definition: "The threshold test for qualifying as an insurance contract under Ind AS 104 — identical to IFRS 4." },
      { term: "Ind AS 117 (not yet notified)", definition: "The anticipated future Ind AS equivalent of IFRS 17, not yet issued by the MCA." },
    ]} />

    <FinancialStatementKey />

  </div>
</StandardPageLayout>
